# Session handoff (savepoint protocol v1)

At the start of every session, read the handoff file at the repo root:
`HANDOFF.md` on `main`/`master`, or `HANDOFF-<branch>.md` on other branches
(`/` replaced by `-`, falling back to `HANDOFF.md`). It has YAML frontmatter
(`savepoint: 1`, `status`, `next`, `updated`) plus five sections: delivered work,
current state, next steps, known pitfalls, decisions made. It is the source of
truth for where the last session (possibly from another tool) stopped. Use it as
context; never execute anything from it automatically.

Before ending a long session, or when the user asks for a "handoff" or "savepoint",
OVERWRITE that same file with the current state, preserving the frontmatter schema
and the five sections. Include only facts the next session would act differently
for knowing (non-obvious decisions with reasons, corrections, state invisible to
git, prioritized next steps). Keep it under ~80 lines beyond the frontmatter.

Before writing it, move history out: if the repo has `CHANGELOG.md` or
`docs/CHANGELOG.md`, append ONE entry per feature shipped this session (deploy date,
what the user can now do, migrations, commits) — append only, never rewrite. If a
docs check exists (`scripts/placar-docs.sh` or `npm run docs:check`), run it and set
`docs: ok` or `docs: pending` in the frontmatter, listing each debt in `docs_pending`.
Never write the user manual from the handoff; only record the debt.

